1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:13,508
January in Nagano's Yokoyu Gorge. You rest as a volcanic boulder on the rim of Jigokudani's thermal pool.

2
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:32,405
Through geothermal vapour, Japanese macaques — the world's northernmost non-human primates — materialize in the snow.

3
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,800
This hot-spring tradition began here in nineteen sixty-three with a single young female and spread culturally through the troop.

4
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,421
Look around the pool at head height. Females and infants sit immersed to the shoulders with half-closed eyes.

5
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:37,221
In twenty eighteen, Kyoto University primatologist Rafaela Takeshita and colleagues measured stress hormones in this exact troop.

6
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,582
Published in the journal Primates, their study proved that winter bathing significantly lowers fecal glucocorticoid levels.

7
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,256
Hot water reduces thermoregulatory energy demand without interrupting nursing for mothers clasping infants.

8
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,335
Turn to your right, just beside the rock edge. Two related adult females engage in unhurried allogrooming.

9
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:06,419
Parting dense two-layered winter pelage removes ectoparasites while releasing endogenous beta-endorphins that cement alliances.

10
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,430
Notice they groom dry shoulders above the waterline so wet fur will not freeze when they step ashore.

11
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,223
Now scan the snowy bank beyond the pool. Several troop members remain completely dry in sub-zero air.

12
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:14,666
Japanese macaque society is strictly matrilineal: dominant females and their kin monopolize this scarce thermal resource.

13
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,451
Lower-ranking females and peripheral males rely instead on metabolic heat and tight winter huddling on the bank.

14
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,542
Snow absorbs every distant echo in the gorge, leaving only mineral water and quiet breathing.

15
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:21,888
Observe free-ranging Japanese macaques live today on the Awaji Island Monkey Center camera at Digital Live Zoo.
